  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 217 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nThis Morning: Frost in North/West-Central Wisconsin \n\nTemperatures are cooling fairly rapidly in cold drainage locations \nof central WI tonight with Black River Falls, WI observing a \ntemperature of 38F just before midnight. With limited sky cover and \ncalm winds overhead, thinking that temperatures in cold drainage \nlocations in Jackson, Monroe, Juneau and Adams counties will likely \nplummet quickly overnight. Based on current observations, would seem \nfairly likely some spots will hit or fall below the freezing mark in \nthese counties. Elsewhere in the Frost Advisory, scattered cloud \ncover further north may hinder some radiational cooling in Clark and \nTaylor counties, however thinking that temperatures should get cool \nenough for frost formation near daybreak. \n\nThis Afternoon - Friday: Dry and Slightly Above Normal Temperatures\n\nSynoptic scale northwesterly flow remains the primary synoptic flow \nthroughout this week that will drive much of how conditions will pan \nout through Friday. Overall, expecting a slight warming trend into \nTuesday as shortwave ridging pushes north of the region with low-\nlevel southerly flow in place. Consequently, temperatures will \nlikely warm into the 70s for much of the local area as seen in \nmedian temperatures in the NBM. However, given the synoptic setup \nand decent mixing with steep hydro-lapse rates atop the mixed layer, \nthinking that temperatures will likely overachieve as we work drier \nair to the surface on Tuesday. Therefore, have decided to nudge high \ntemperatures closer to the 90th percentile of the NBM for Tuesday \nafternoon with highs in the middle 70s. In any case, this ridging \nslides eastward in the northwesterly flow which in turn returns flow \nback to west/northwesterly through Friday and thus keeps \ntemperatures closer to normal with highs generally in the 60s to \nlower 70s. With the lack of any appreciable shortwave energy moving \nthrough the northwesterly flow throughout the upcoming week, \nthinking any precipitation potential is very low which is seconded \nby the grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) keeping measurable \nprecipitation probabilities very low (0-20% chance) through Friday. \n\nThis Weekend: Trending Dry and Warm\n\nAs we head into the weekend, guidance generally agrees on pushing \nsome broader upper-level ridging into the region and turning flow \nmore zonal aloft. This in addition to increasing southerly low-level \nflow will aid in advecting a warmer airmass into the region for the \nweekend. Consequently, the NBM has fairly respectable probabilities \n(30-70%) for seeing high temperatures over 75 degrees this weekend, \nprimarily on Sunday as the ridge moves overhead. Additionally, \nensemble guidance generally agrees on keeping measurable \nprecipitation chances to a minimum (10-30%) over the weekend with \nthe grand ensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble). However, noting some \ngeopotential height falls in deterministic guidance by Sunday and \ninto early next week as a large scale trough attempts to eject into \nthe Great Plains which could increase precipitation chances, but \npredictability remains very low during this time period.",
